    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-4108",
      "problem": "The point $A$ with coordinates $(2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y = f(x)$ and the point $B$ with coordinates $(2,-4)$ lies on the curve $y = g(x).$ If the curve $y = g(x)$ is related to $y = f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x) = f(x) + k$, then what is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the point $B(2, -4)$ lies $3$ units *below* the point $A(2, -1).$ So, the graph of $y = g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units *down*. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14218283/assets/4656ae5ff75132fc9b760b6a8f73b0b434a8f66ecd621fa974a265df1d0d8a2c.png)\n\nAs a result, $k = -3.$",
